{"text": "Like all historians, social historians draw from a variety of primary sources. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Social history often emphasizes ordinary people rather than elites. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Yet, in most places and times, non elites lacked access to education and, thus, did not produce substantial amounts of documentation. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Even when they did produce documentation, these materials rarely ended up in archives where they could be preserved and made available to researchers. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Because of such biases in the historical record, social historians have to be very creative in their work. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Like other historians, social historians analyze textual sources produced by government officials, employers, and other elites. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Yet rather than simply take such sources at face value, they read them against the grain. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> So, for example, a social historian will read legal documents not only for the facts provided by slaveowners, she will also examine the silences, interruptions, rumors, and other details that offer hints about the enslaved peoples attitudes and actions.", "alignment": {"characters": ["L", "i", "k", "e", " ", "a", "l", "l", " ", "h", "i", "s", "t", "o", "r", "i", "a", "n", "s", ",", " ", "s", "o", "c", "i", "a", "l", " ", "h", "i", "s", "t", "o", "r", "i", "a", "n", "s", " ", "d", "r", "a", "w", " ", "f", "r", "o", "m", " ", "a", " ", "v", "a", "r", "i", "e", "t", "y", " ", "o", "f", " ", "p", "r", "i", "m", "a", "r", "y", " ", "s", "o", "u", "r", "c", "e", "s", ".", " ", "<", "b", "r", "e", "a", "k", " ", "t", "i", "m", "e", "=", "\"", "0", ".", "8", "s", "\"", " ", "/", ">", " ", "S", "o", "c", "i", "a", "l", " ", "h", "i", "s", "t", "o", "r", "y", " ", "o", "f", "t", "e", "n", " ", "e", "m", "p", "h", "a", "s", "i", "z", "e", "s", " ", "o", "r", "d", "i", "n", "a", "r", "y", " ", "p", "e", "o", "p", "l", "e", " ", "r", "a", "t", "h", "e", "r", " "